CD(1)                         Funny CLI Manual                         CD(1)

NAME
    cd — Change the current working directory, because apparently
         walking there wasn't an option.

SYNOPSIS
    cd [path]

DESCRIPTION
    The 'cd' command changes the current working directory to the
    specified path.

    If no path is provided, 'cd' returns you to the CLI workspace,
    presumably because you've wandered far enough.

    The command uses the path resolver to turn the target into an
    absolute path before attempting the journey.

ARGUMENTS
    path
        The directory you would like to travel to.

        Optional. If omitted, the CLI workspace becomes the destination.

BEHAVIOR
    With a path:

        > cd projects
            Moves into 'projects'.

    Without a path:

        > cd
            Returns to the CLI workspace.

    If something goes wrong, the command prints the error rather than
    pretending everything is fine.

ERRORS
    cd error: <error>
        The requested journey has been cancelled.

        Possible causes include:
        - The path does not exist.
        - You do not have permission to enter it.
        - The filesystem has decided today is not your day.
        - Something else went catastrophically sideways.

EXAMPLES
    > cd src
        Enters the 'src' directory.

        A short trip. Probably.

    > cd /tmp
        Goes directly to /tmp.

        Your files may or may not survive the experience.

    > cd
        Returns to the CLI workspace.

        Home sweet home.

    > cd definitely-not-here
        cd error: [some filesystem complaint]

        The directory has chosen not to participate.

FUN FACTS
    - 'cd' changes the process's current working directory.
    - It accepts exactly zero or one path from the CLI's perspective.
    - Unlike 'ls', there are no aliases. There is only 'cd'.
    - Running 'cd' with no arguments is the CLI equivalent of
      "take me home."

SEE ALSO
    ls(1), pwd(1), mkdir(1), rm(1)

